{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.16,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.16)=0.40.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.04,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.04)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.16$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.16}{0.04}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.40$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.40}{0.005}=80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.161… \\\\ p & ≈2.161….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89f04859e5190bec7772e9903ae2b7cbd3166bfcd0e1be7aa18e9cf5c9f84e31"} {"question" "q-334645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.15,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.15)=0.54.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.003.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.05}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.54$ to $0.003.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.54}{0.003}=180.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈180 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(180) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.726… \\\\ p & ≈3.726….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89f04859e5190bec7772e9903ae2b7cbd3166bfcd0e1be7aa18e9cf5c9f84e31"} {"question" "q-334647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89f04859e5190bec7772e9903ae2b7cbd3166bfcd0e1be7aa18e9cf5c9f84e31"} {"question" "q-334753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89f04859e5190bec7772e9903ae2b7cbd3166bfcd0e1be7aa18e9cf5c9f84e31"} {"question" "q-334756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.24$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.24)≈0.32.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.12,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.12)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.12.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.24}{0.12}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={2}^{4}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.12,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.12)≈\\frac{0.32}{16}=0.02.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89f04859e5190bec7772e9903ae2b7cbd3166bfcd0e1be7aa18e9cf5c9f84e31"} {"question" "q-334951" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"} {"question" "q-334951" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.02"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c0e860d8f2088725a684a641697d37f7118e4d0dc11a0b61bf3222d49f92e"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2543 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
